### Use custom PostgreSQL types in a pgTable definition

Source: https://github.com/drizzle-team/drizzle-orm/blob/0.36.4/docs/custom-types.lite.md

This example shows how to use the custom types defined above in a `pgTable` definition. It demonstrates applying the custom types to columns with methods like `.primaryKey()`, `.notNull()`, `.default()`, and passing configuration options to `customTimestamp`.

```typescript
const usersTable = pgTable('users', {
  id: customSerial('id').primaryKey(),
  name: customText('name').notNull(),
  verified: customBoolean('verified').notNull().default(false),
  jsonb: customJsonb<string[]>('jsonb'),
  createdAt: customTimestamp('created_at', { withTimezone: true }).notNull()
    .default(sql`now()`),
});
```

--------------------------------

### Select with filtering, partial select, and ordering

Source: https://github.com/drizzle-team/drizzle-orm/blob/0.36.4/drizzle-orm/src/sqlite-core/README.md

Basic select queries with filtering, partial select, limit/offset, and ordering. Uses better-sqlite3 driver.

```typescript
import { sqliteTable, text, integer } from 'drizzle-orm/sqlite-core';
import { and, asc, desc, eq, or } from 'drizzle-orm'
import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/better-sqlite3';
import Database from 'better-sqlite3';

const users = sqliteTable('users', {
  id: integer('id').primaryKey(),
  name: text('full_name'),
});

const sqlite = new Database('sqlite.db');
const db = drizzle(sqlite);

db.select().from(users).all();
db.select().from(users).where(eq(users.id, 42)).get();

// you can combine filters with and(...) or or(...)
db.select().from(users).where(and(eq(users.id, 42), eq(users.name, 'Dan'))).all();

db.select().from(users).where(or(eq(users.id, 42), eq(users.id, 1))).all();

// partial select
const result = db
  .select({
    field1: users.id,
    field2: users.name,
  })
  .from(users)
  .all();
const { field1, field2 } = result[0];

// limit offset & order by
db.select().from(users).limit(10).offset(10).all();
db.select().from(users).orderBy(users.name).all();
db.select().from(users).orderBy(desc(users.name)).all();
// you can pass multiple order args
db.select().from(users).orderBy(asc(users.name), desc(users.name)).all();
```

--------------------------------

### Common way of defining custom types > Examples > Postgres Data Types using `node-postgres` driver

Source: https://github.com/drizzle-team/drizzle-orm/blob/0.36.4/docs/custom-types.lite.md

#### Usage for all types will be same as defined functions in Drizzle ORM

```typescript
const usersTable = pgTable('users', {
  id: customSerial('id').primaryKey(),
  name: customText('name').notNull(),
  verified: customBoolean('verified').notNull().default(false),
  jsonb: customJsonb<string[]>('jsonb'),
  createdAt: customTimestamp('created_at', { withTimezone: true }).notNull()
    .default(sql`now()`),
});
```

--------------------------------

### Define custom PostgreSQL data types with customType

Source: https://github.com/drizzle-team/drizzle-orm/blob/0.36.4/docs/custom-types.lite.md

These are examples of defining custom PostgreSQL data types using the `customType` function from Drizzle ORM with the `node-postgres` driver. Each example shows how to define a custom type for serial, text, boolean, jsonb, and timestamp columns. The `customJsonb` and `customTimestamp` examples include custom `toDriver` and `fromDriver` conversions.

```typescript
const customSerial = customType<{ data: number; notNull: true; default: true }>(
  {
    dataType() {
      return 'serial';
    },
  },
);
```

```typescript
const customText = customType<{ data: string }>({
  dataType() {
    return 'text';
  },
});
```

```typescript
const customBoolean = customType<{ data: boolean }>({
  dataType() {
    return 'boolean';
  },
});
```

```typescript
const customJsonb = <TData>(name: string) =>
  customType<{ data: TData; driverData: string }>({
    dataType() {
      return 'jsonb';
    },
    toDriver(value: TData): string {
      return JSON.stringify(value);
    },
  })(name);
```

```typescript
const customTimestamp = customType<
  {
    data: Date;
    driverData: string;
    config: { withTimezone: boolean; precision?: number };
  }
>({
  dataType(config) {
    const precision = typeof config.precision !== 'undefined'
      ? ` (${config.precision})`
      : '';
    return `timestamp${precision}${
      config.withTimezone ? ' with time zone' : ''
    }`;
  },
  fromDriver(value: string): Date {
    return new Date(value);
  },
});
```

--------------------------------

### Define PostgreSQL table with $onUpdate and $onUpdateFn

Source: https://github.com/drizzle-team/drizzle-orm/blob/0.36.4/changelogs/drizzle-orm/0.30.5.md

Defines a PostgreSQL table with columns using the new $onUpdate and $onUpdateFn methods. The updateCounter column increments on update, updatedAt sets the current date, and alwaysNull sets null on update. The function is also called on insert if no default is provided.

```ts
const usersOnUpdate = pgTable('users_on_update', {
  id: serial('id').primaryKey(),
  name: text('name').notNull(),
  updateCounter: integer('update_counter').default(sql`1`).$onUpdateFn(() => sql`update_counter + 1`),
  updatedAt: timestamp('updated_at', { mode: 'date', precision: 3 }).$onUpdate(() => new Date()),
  alwaysNull: text('always_null').$type<string | null>().$onUpdate(() => null),
});
```

